PROCESS: Propane Propylene Stream with Light Impurities through 4A
VERSION: 1.0
ONLINE_LOOKUP: false
THERMO_METHOD: PR
DESCRIPTION: Competitive adsorption of a C3 vapor containing CO2, methane and nitrogen on fresh 4A molecular sieve.

COMPONENTS:
    propane | Propane | MW=44.09562
    propylene | Propylene | MW=42.07974
    CO2 | Carbon dioxide | MW=44.0095
    CH4 | Methane | MW=16.04246
    N2 | Nitrogen | MW=28.0134

STREAM Feed : FEED -> MS.feed
    T = 313 [K]
    P = 1 [bar]
    F = 1 [kmol/h]
    x = propane:0.85, propylene:0.10, CO2:0.03, CH4:0.01, N2:0.01

STREAM Product : MS.product -> PRODUCT
STREAM Adsorbate : MS.adsorbate -> PRODUCT

UNIT MS : MolecularSieveDryer
    sieve_type = 4A
    adsorbent_mass_flow = 50 [kg/h]

# One isothermal equilibrium contact; 50 kg/h is fresh/regenerated dry-sieve
# throughput, not a fixed-bed inventory or a breakthrough specification.
# All five species have built-in isotherms and compete through IAST.
# CO2 removal comes with hydrocarbon loss, particularly propylene uptake.
# Methane and nitrogen are weakly adsorbed; this is not complete light-gas
# cleanup. Compare component molar flows as well as product mole fractions.
#
# Data limitation: the propane/propylene curves were measured at 423-473 K;
# their use at 313 K is temperature extrapolation. IAST fictitious pure
# fugacities can also leave the measured ranges. Keep the runtime warnings:
# this example illustrates model behavior, not a validated separation design.
# Adsorbate is a material-accounting outlet for uptake on the solid.
# Physical cooling duty includes adsorption; sieve regeneration is not modeled.
